Using Custom Keys {#customKeys}
============================

This page is meant to guide you through the usage of a custom key with a cache.

## Key Requirements

* Must be move-constructible.
* Must implement equality operator (`bool operator==(const Self&) const;`).
* Must be hashable by the hasher passed as template parameter to the cache (by default, `absl::Hash<Key>`).

These requirements are checked by the `cachemere::CacheKey` concept.
The key does _not_ need to be copyable, nor assignable: keys with `const` members are fine.

## Usage Example

### With `absl::Hash` (default hasher)

```cpp
#include <string>

#include <cachemere.h>

struct ComplexKey {
    std::string first;
    std::string second;

    bool operator==(const ComplexKey& other) const = default;

    /// Override for the type to be hashable by `absl::Hash`.
    template<typename H> friend H AbslHashValue(H h, const ComplexKey& s)
    {
        return H::combine(std::move(h), s.first, s.second);
    }

    /// For use with cachemere::measurement::CapacityDynamicallyAllocated.
    size_t capacity() const
    {
        return first.capacity() + second.capacity();
    }
};

using ValueT = int;

using Cache = cachemere::presets::memory::LRUCache<ComplexKey,
                                                   ValueT,
                                                   cachemere::measurement::SizeOf<ValueT>,
                                                   cachemere::measurement::CapacityDynamicallyAllocated<ComplexKey>>;
```

### With a custom hasher

```cpp
#include <string>

#include <cachemere.h>

struct ComplexKey {
    std::string first;
    std::string second;

    bool operator==(const ComplexKey& other) const = default;

    /// For use with cachemere::measurement::CapacityDynamicallyAllocated.
    size_t capacity() const
    {
        return first.capacity() + second.capacity();
    }
};

struct VeryBadHash {
    size_t operator()(const ComplexKey&) const
    {
        return 0;
    }
};

using ValueT = int;

using Cache = cachemere::presets::memory::LRUCache<ComplexKey,
                                                   ValueT,
                                                   cachemere::measurement::SizeOf<ValueT>,
                                                   cachemere::measurement::CapacityDynamicallyAllocated<ComplexKey>,
                                                   VeryBadHash>;
```

### With `cachemere::CompositeKey`

`cachemere::CompositeKey` builds a key out of several components, and takes care of equality and hashing.
Its `size()` sums the sizes of its components, so the default `cachemere::measurement::Size` measures it.

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

#include <cachemere.h>

using Key   = cachemere::CompositeKey<std::string, std::uint32_t>;
using Cache = cachemere::presets::memory::LRUCache<Key, std::string>;

int main()
{
    Cache cache{1024};
    cache.insert({"user", 42U}, "some value");

    return cache.contains(Key{"user", 42U}) ? 0 : 1;
}
```

Strings, string views and C strings are hashed by content: a `CompositeKey` of `std::string_view` can look up a
`CompositeKey` of `std::string`, see \ref heterogeneousLookup "Heterogeneous Lookup".
